76
00:05:04,415 --> 00:05:05,400
Scott: that's really interesting.

77
00:05:07,002 --> 00:05:08,526
Rym: I learned about it just now.

78
00:05:08,626 --> 00:05:17,240
Scott: I mean, the story is from- First it's been heard about or whatever, but basically here's the idea.

79
00:05:17,601 --> 00:05:19,068
Scott: You got a VPN, right?

80
00:05:19,088 --> 00:05:26,180
Scott: You're using VPN software and what a VPN does, you can listen to our show on VPNs, is all the packets from your computer, right?

81
00:05:26,440 --> 00:05:31,320
Scott: You encrypt them and you send them directly to another computer somewhere else on the internet.

82
00:05:32,221 --> 00:05:41,380
Scott: And then that can, you're basically, instead of being on the network you're on, even though you are on the network you're on, you're virtually on the network.

83
00:05:41,601 --> 00:05:43,448
Scott: the other computer is on, right?

84
00:05:43,548 --> 00:05:52,365
Scott: Because it's the part that's exposed to the world and you're just directly sending and receiving all your packets to that one other computer and it's encrypted.

85
00:05:52,706 --> 00:05:57,299
Scott: So the network you're on doesn't just sees you sending encrypted, it becomes a dumb pipe.

86
00:05:57,861 --> 00:05:58,142
Rym: Yeah.

87
00:05:58,223 --> 00:06:09,920
Rym: Oh, well, like imagine if you did this with water, literally like if I have my sink and I just stuck a pipe to one of my water fixtures and ran it down the street all the way to Scott's apartment and into his apartment.

88
00:06:10,221 --> 00:06:11,265
Scott: And the drain too.

89
00:06:11,345 --> 00:06:15,660
Scott: So the drain comes back and goes into my drain and the water comes from my water.

90
00:06:15,960 --> 00:06:19,900
Rym: And anyone looking at my water would just, Scott's water is mixed in with my water.

91
00:06:19,900 --> 00:06:20,181
Scott: Right.

92
00:06:20,282 --> 00:06:23,214
Scott: Rym's water bill would be zero, right?

93
00:06:23,314 --> 00:06:27,116
Scott: And my water bill would be high and my drain would get backed up with Rym sewage.

94
00:06:27,659 --> 00:06:27,820
Scott: Anyway.

95
00:06:27,980 --> 00:06:29,460
Rym: That hasn't happened in a while, thankfully.

96
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:30,681
Scott: Anyway.

97
00:06:30,701 --> 00:06:32,326
Scott: Oh, that's good.

98
00:06:33,249 --> 00:06:51,752
Scott: So here's the thing is you most often use VPNs for piracy because you virtually put yourself in another country to watch videos that you can't watch where you live, or you use it because you are on some dodgy ass network, like a coffee shop or a school or some building you're in and some business.

99
00:06:51,833 --> 00:06:52,033
Scott: Right.

100
00:06:52,094 --> 00:06:57,260
Scott: And you, or you, you, you're working from home and you don't, you don't even trust your home network.

101
00:06:57,421 --> 00:07:00,640
Scott: You want your business business to go to the company's network only.

102
00:07:00,740 --> 00:07:06,323
Scott: So you convert VPN to the company's network and it lets you access the internal company network stuff that's not public.

103
00:07:06,364 --> 00:07:06,524
Scott: Right.

104
00:07:06,585 --> 00:07:09,959
Scott: Because you're virtually on that network instead of on here.

105
00:07:09,999 --> 00:07:10,140
Scott: Right.

106
00:07:10,541 --> 00:07:12,066
Scott: So here's the thing.

107
00:07:12,086 --> 00:07:18,675
Scott: Uh, according to this article, uh, you go to a coffee shop, let's say, right?

108
00:07:18,715 --> 00:07:22,818
Scott: The one time, the, the use case where you most commonly want to use a VPN.

109
00:07:22,999 --> 00:07:23,160
Scott: Right.

110
00:07:23,882 --> 00:07:33,199
Scott: And even though you're going to use a VPN and you're not going to really use their network, you still are going to connect to their wifi probably and their DHCP server will give you an IP.

111
00:07:33,701 --> 00:07:35,348
Scott: That's all that stuff is still going to happen.

112
00:07:35,469 --> 00:07:38,160
Scott: And then you establish your VPN connection.

113
00:07:38,341 --> 00:07:45,752
Scott: So that does leave a small window where that network, if it was malicious, could do something to you.

114
00:07:46,013 --> 00:07:46,314
Scott: Right.

115
00:07:46,957 --> 00:07:54,209
Scott: Um, you know, if you send some traffic before the VPNs up, let's say they might see it or, you know, who knows what's going to happen.

116
00:07:54,430 --> 00:07:54,590
Scott: Right.

117
00:07:55,113 --> 00:07:55,916
Scott: So here's the thing.

118
00:07:55,976 --> 00:08:08,852
Scott: According to this, the DHCP server can send instructions that will tell your computer to change its routing table, uh, including telling it to route.

119
00:08:08,892 --> 00:08:10,120
Rym: I haven't read this article yet.

120
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:18,936
Rym: That triggered a lot of extremely dire thoughts because I used to administer like IBM's DHCP servers.

121
00:08:19,016 --> 00:08:29,904
Scott: I know that depending on your computer, if the network you're on is malicious, they could basically have you route some traffic or all traffic to another server.

122
00:08:29,944 --> 00:08:34,179
Scott: For example, their DHCP server, the one that just told you what your IP address is.

123
00:08:34,441 --> 00:08:35,144
Rym: It's option 121.

124
00:08:35,144 --> 00:08:36,089
Rym: That's it.

125
00:08:36,431 --> 00:08:38,039
Rym: I'm trying to remember if I've ever used this.

126
00:08:39,080 --> 00:08:47,655
Scott: And so then what would happen is unbeknownst to you, as long as they keep, you know, sending that traffic to the public internet, right?

127
00:08:48,379 --> 00:09:01,060
Scott: You, unless you look carefully or unless you're like having trouble connecting to like some internal service you were expecting to connect to that would only be accessible by your company VPN, right?

128
00:09:01,561 --> 00:09:05,216
Scott: You might not realize anything's wrong because you didn't actually look to see that.

129
00:09:05,256 --> 00:09:08,891
Scott: you just see that your VPN is on and you trust that and that's good enough for you, right?

130
00:09:08,911 --> 00:09:13,259
Scott: You're not opening up your packet inspector every single time you connect to at a coffee shop.

131
00:09:13,320 --> 00:09:17,820
Rym: So I don't know when this was last updated, but I'm just going to point out that I am looking at literally today.

132
00:09:18,161 --> 00:09:18,563
Rym: No, no, no.

133
00:09:18,583 --> 00:09:23,200
Rym: I'm looking at the RFC for the actual documentation for that option.

134
00:09:23,421 --> 00:09:25,249
Rym: Option 121, like the thing at issue.

135
00:09:25,812 --> 00:09:27,460
Rym: And it has a section right there.

136
00:09:27,900 --> 00:09:33,940
Rym: Security considerations, potential exposures to attack at the DHCP protocol are discussed in this section.

137
00:09:34,801 --> 00:09:35,002
Scott: Yep.

138
00:09:36,527 --> 00:09:45,220
Scott: And so what would happen is basically the one time you want to use a VPN, you're most likely to use it when you want to get some internet juice from an untrustworthy network.

139
00:09:46,062 --> 00:09:50,400
Scott: If it's an untrustworthy, malicious, intelligent network, they could be using this attack.

140
00:09:50,500 --> 00:10:01,900
Scott: They say it's been available since 2002 to basically you think you're using your VPN, but you're not and some or all of your traffic is routed, then they can see it and you might not realize it.

141
00:10:02,600 --> 00:10:03,282
Rym: So I guess what's?

142
00:10:04,105 --> 00:10:14,120
Rym: a few things stand out to me because one, all modern traffic, anything you're doing, VPN or not, should already either not matter or be TLS encrypted.

143
00:10:14,541 --> 00:10:16,788
Rym: Like, yeah, your content should be safe.

144
00:10:17,130 --> 00:10:23,900
Rym: And two, using a VPN for privacy is kind of stupid because now you're just trusting whoever owns the other end of the VPN to not.

145
00:10:24,140 --> 00:10:26,053
Scott: Well, the other end of the VPN could be you as well.

146
00:10:26,114 --> 00:10:26,919
Scott: And then you could trust it.

147
00:10:27,101 --> 00:10:28,640
Rym: Sure, then you can in that rare case.

148
00:10:28,941 --> 00:10:31,512
Scott: That is a separate VPN discussion.

149
00:10:31,552 --> 00:10:33,300
Scott: We're talking about this issue specifically.

150
00:10:34,561 --> 00:10:40,540
Scott: I think we have a pretty easy solution, which is either A, don't allow your routing tables to be changed.

151
00:10:40,640 --> 00:10:46,060
Scott: But what if there is actually a real use case where you need it on like a company network or something?

152
00:10:46,681 --> 00:10:48,347
Scott: You won't need it on a public Internet.

153
00:10:48,508 --> 00:10:51,900
Scott: It's like a normal coffee shop isn't going to need that, right?

154
00:10:52,400 --> 00:11:06,360
Scott: Or two, I think there's an easy mitigation, which is even if you allow it, every VPN software out there could just do a simple check of sending some packets and then verifying it if they were VPNed or not.

155
00:11:06,440 --> 00:11:08,769
Rym: Yeah, a little more context in through how it looks like this works.

156
00:11:08,849 --> 00:11:11,580
Rym: So it can route some or all of the traffic.

157
00:11:11,660 --> 00:11:12,785
Rym: And I think that's the key point.

158
00:11:12,825 --> 00:11:13,688
Rym: It can be selective.

159
00:11:14,070 --> 00:11:16,319
Rym: So like it could route all the websites you look at.

160
00:11:16,941 --> 00:11:21,259
Scott: Or they could just be like, hey, if anyone does any Facebook logins, route them here.

161
00:11:22,160 --> 00:11:26,320
Rym: But it can't compromise the traffic itself.

162
00:11:26,620 --> 00:11:32,620
Rym: All it really does is de-anonymize it or prevent it from getting where it's supposed to go.

163
00:11:32,660 --> 00:11:42,220
Rym: Because if it goes over the public Internet to a place that isn't accessible to the public Internet, then it'll just fail because there's no route to wherever it's trying to get to except through the fully realized VPN.

164
00:11:42,941 --> 00:11:50,100
Rym: Doesn't seem like this is super dire except for people who are trying to use VPNs for privacy as opposed to security.

165
00:11:50,820 --> 00:11:57,280
Scott: It's just really a fascinating situation where you can have your routing table change and not realize it.

166
00:11:57,440 --> 00:12:06,771
Rym: The first comment on the article actually is like, I think it's important to clarify that this affects VPNs that are used for anonymizing traffic, not really any other use case for VPNs.

167
00:12:07,252 --> 00:12:09,420
Rym: I don't see this as a huge vulnerability.

168
00:12:10,301 --> 00:12:17,520
Scott: But I think what's huge about it is that the time it can happen is the time you're most likely to use a VPN.

169
00:12:17,960 --> 00:12:21,436
Scott: If you're doing it at home, your DHCP server isn't malicious.

170
00:12:21,456 --> 00:12:22,200
Scott: You're going to be fine.

171
00:12:22,521 --> 00:12:26,798
Scott: If you do it at your company, your DHCP server at your company isn't malicious.

172
00:12:26,858 --> 00:12:27,220
Scott: It's fine.

173
00:12:28,081 --> 00:12:31,880
Scott: But you're really probably not even using a VPN so much when you're at your company.

174
00:12:32,040 --> 00:12:33,225
Rym: You're already on the network.

175
00:12:33,266 --> 00:12:36,400
Scott: But when you go to a coffee shop, that's when you're at an airport.

176
00:12:36,520 --> 00:12:37,585
Scott: That's when you want to use it.

177
00:12:37,685 --> 00:12:41,460
Scott: And that's when the DHCP server isn't trustworthy.

178
00:12:41,921 --> 00:12:44,200
Rym: That is the exact time and place I would use a VPN.

179
00:12:44,800 --> 00:12:51,500
Scott: You can't avoid the DHCP server because no matter how untrustworthy the network is, you need an IP address to get any Internet.

180
00:12:52,183 --> 00:12:57,942
Rym: Apparently Android is not affected by it because Android never implemented option 121.

181
00:12:57,942 --> 00:13:02,280
Scott: Not every computer is going to be affected depending on the DHCP client.

182
00:13:03,080 --> 00:13:05,168
Rym: I think this is going to be earth-shattering.

183
00:13:05,328 --> 00:13:12,040
Rym: only for people who think that VPNs protect their privacy, which I still think is not true in almost any circumstance.

184
00:13:12,680 --> 00:13:13,745
Scott: Depends on the VPN.

185
00:13:14,348 --> 00:13:17,020
Scott: I'm sure some of them are trustworthy and aren't lying.

186
00:13:19,122 --> 00:13:22,151
Scott: And I'm sure some people are VPN-ing to themselves.

187
00:13:22,172 --> 00:13:24,920
Scott: They just cloud-host a machine and VPN to it.

188
00:13:25,841 --> 00:13:36,000
Rym: But if your goal is just to hide what websites you're going to as opposed to some other use case, it's not usually an important use of VPN, except in specific circumstances.

