Rym: That is literally what's in the template.

469
00:29:17,980 --> 00:29:18,607
Scott: I guess so.

470
00:29:20,410 --> 00:29:29,410
Rym: So yeah, Scott, you have had what I would characterize as insane monitor problems recently, multiple times.

471
00:29:29,850 --> 00:29:29,910
Scott: Yes.

472
00:29:30,091 --> 00:29:35,470
Scott: It's weird, because my feeling overall is that I have a lot less breakages than other people have.

473
00:29:35,710 --> 00:29:44,427
Rym: Well, you also have a tendency to, whenever anyone else's things break, kind of blame them for it and remind everyone, "My shit never breaks.

474
00:29:44,467 --> 00:29:45,309
Rym: That never happens to

475
00:29:45,329 --> 00:29:45,409
Rym: me.".

476
00:29:45,750 --> 00:29:50,179
Rym: And yet when things happen to you, it's great, because none of us even call you on it.

477
00:29:50,219 --> 00:29:55,550
Rym: We just watch you post and post and post about the insane problem you're having.

478
00:29:56,250 --> 00:29:56,350
Scott: Right.

479
00:29:56,370 --> 00:30:08,230
Scott: Well, the thing is, is the problems I have are always insane problems, because I firmly believe that any software-related problem, there isn't a software-related breakage that I can't fix.

480
00:30:09,012 --> 00:30:10,335
Scott: I don't think that exists, right?

481
00:30:10,937 --> 00:30:15,630
Scott: As long as I have another computer that works, and the internet, and some tools.

482
00:30:16,510 --> 00:30:21,180
Rym: I mean, we're going to ignore the... There was an era where I only had one computer and no other way to get on the internet.

483
00:30:21,200 --> 00:30:25,790
Rym: If that computer was broken, I had to go find someone else with a computer to help me.

484
00:30:26,330 --> 00:30:26,811
Scott: That's right.

485
00:30:27,051 --> 00:30:36,230
Scott: But I strongly believe there isn't a software-related breakage that I could not get around somehow and get back up to speed.

486
00:30:37,091 --> 00:30:47,330
Scott: So, most of the time I'm doing those criticisms of other people, it's like they have a software-related problem, and it's most often, like 90% of the time, I'm criticizing them, not because their shit broke.

487
00:30:47,550 --> 00:30:48,873
Scott: That happens, right?

488
00:30:49,233 --> 00:30:54,825
Scott: But because their shit broke because they were fucking around, right?

489
00:30:54,885 --> 00:30:56,748
Scott: It's like, "Oh, they're installing some weird

490
00:30:56,768 --> 00:30:57,530
Scott: softwares!".

491
00:30:57,791 --> 00:31:02,302
Scott: Or, "Doing some weird configurations and non-default settings and trying to be

492
00:31:02,362 --> 00:31:02,944
Scott: fancy!".

493
00:31:03,024 --> 00:31:05,510
Scott: It's like, yeah, that's why your shit broke, you're trying to be fancy.

494
00:31:05,670 --> 00:31:10,561
Scott: If you just keep it simple, don't fuck around, use your default stuff, right?

495
00:31:10,761 --> 00:31:14,630
Scott: Then your shit is much less likely to break, and that is why my stuff breaks not often.

496
00:31:14,690 --> 00:31:21,456
Scott: But, even if you are expert at software problem avoidance like I am, hardware just breaks.

497
00:31:21,536 --> 00:31:36,550
Scott: We did shows about repairing it, but sometimes you can't, or sometimes it's too difficult, or sometimes no one even knows what the fuck's wrong, as it has happened with my monitors recently.

498
00:31:38,271 --> 00:31:42,957
Scott: So, one monitor had this problem that I finally was able to diagnose.

499
00:31:42,977 --> 00:31:53,050
Scott: What was happening was that, mostly at night, and mostly after watching video, there would be these vertical bars on the monitor.

500
00:31:53,270 --> 00:31:57,690
Scott: Not like, it wasn't like bars, like jail cell, where you couldn't see half the picture.

501
00:31:58,111 --> 00:32:01,270
Rym: Yeah, and it wasn't like burn-in from anything on your screen?

502
00:32:02,071 --> 00:32:02,712
Scott: No, it wasn't.

503
00:32:02,772 --> 00:32:14,785
Scott: It was these vertical alternating bars where you could see, mostly at the edges of the monitor, you could see it like this repeating pattern.

504
00:32:14,805 --> 00:32:19,610
Scott: The middle of the monitor was mostly fine, and it was really only visible in grey areas.

505
00:32:19,670 --> 00:32:23,937
Scott: If you put on a different color, like red, you couldn't see it.

506
00:32:23,997 --> 00:32:26,241
Scott: It was only if you put it to grey, then you could really see it.

507
00:32:26,481 --> 00:32:31,009
Scott: Light grey, dark grey, light grey, dark grey, straight line vertical bands.

508
00:32:32,531 --> 00:32:33,912
Scott: And it was like, what the hell's going on?

509
00:32:34,513 --> 00:32:43,162
Scott: And eventually, I was able to figure out that it was only happening because the monitor has two different color modes, right?

510
00:32:43,222 --> 00:32:51,010
Scott: There's RGB and YPBR, you know, the other color encoding mode that can happen, right?

511
00:32:51,551 --> 00:32:59,186
Scott: And basically, one is the one that televisions use, and RGB is the one that monitors use, but nothing really stops you from using the other one.

512
00:32:59,207 --> 00:33:00,930
Scott: It's not like there's something wrong with it, right?

513
00:33:01,190 --> 00:33:07,800
Scott: It's like if you plug your computer into a television with the HDMI cable, it's going to use the Y whatever one, with the component one.

514
00:33:08,380 --> 00:33:10,583
Scott: If you plug it into a monitor, it's going to use the RGB one.

515
00:33:10,623 --> 00:33:15,230
Scott: There's a negotiation that happens between the two devices, and my monitor supported both.

516
00:33:15,771 --> 00:33:17,353
Scott: So why shouldn't both of them work?

517
00:33:17,593 --> 00:33:31,650
Scott: What was happening was because the Mac was basically detecting the monitor as like a television because of reasons, and so it was using a different color mode, like a television, which isn't really a problem because the monitor supports it, so it should work.

518
00:33:33,030 --> 00:33:52,150
Scott: But this was only happening when it was in that mode, and it was only happening when areas of the screen were like black for, or like dark for an extended period, and then when I would like, you'd leave like an area of the monitor black for a while, and then put the gray there instead, and then you would see it in that area.

519
00:33:52,871 --> 00:33:58,783
Scott: So it's like I did a test where I made the whole monitor black, and then I waited, and then I came back, and there it was.

520
00:33:59,224 --> 00:34:02,470
Scott: An RGB didn't happen, but in the Y color mode it happened.

521
00:34:04,411 --> 00:34:11,239
Scott: And so then it also happened if I used the monitor's built, like unplug everything from the monitor except the power cable, right?

522
00:34:11,918 --> 00:34:20,989
Scott: If I use the monitor's built-in test screens that just show colors solid, you could still see it, indicating the problem is in the monitor.

523
00:34:21,050 --> 00:34:45,210
Scott: This monitor has a bug with that color mode of some kind, and so the Dell was very nice, and they sent me a newer model, basically the best monitor that they have, for me at least, refurbished, but whatever, and they said put the old one in the box, and it cost me zero dollars, and this monitor is great, it's much better, and it doesn't have that problem.

524
00:34:46,433 --> 00:34:47,469
Scott: So, hooray.

525
00:34:48,610 --> 00:34:55,138
Rym: So my previous laptop, I've owned a few laptops in my whole life, because my philosophy with laptops is the same as with computers.

526
00:34:55,719 --> 00:35:04,850
Rym: I buy the best goddamn laptop, and I use it until I physically cannot anymore, because it has failed, or it is so obsolete it is impossible to use.

527
00:35:05,410 --> 00:35:34,890
Rym: And my previous laptop had an SSD in it, and I had it for a very, very long time, and it worked great, like I wanted to keep it forever, and one day I was in London on a work trip, and I had my laptop open, and I'm like playing a video game on it, and the video game crashes, I don't think much of it, but I figure it's time to go to bed anyway, so like I do a little video call with Emily, and then I go to open up Notepad, because I want to just type a few notes about a thing for the next, like an agenda for the next work meeting, and just like have that ready.

528
00:35:35,492 --> 00:35:36,514
Rym: And Notepad won't open.

529
00:35:37,597 --> 00:35:38,941
Rym: Like, Notepad just won't open.

530
00:35:39,683 --> 00:35:41,267
Rym: When I launch it, nothing happens.

531
00:35:41,768 --> 00:35:42,450
Rym: And I'm like, that's weird.

532
00:35:43,171 --> 00:35:43,830
Rym: You start the computer.

533
00:35:44,110 --> 00:35:45,814
Rym: I reboot.

534
00:35:45,934 --> 00:35:47,117
Rym: Notepad won't open.

535
00:35:47,739 --> 00:35:49,383
Rym: Nothing else is wrong with the computer.

536
00:35:49,864 --> 00:35:52,310
Rym: Literally nothing, but Notepad won't open.

537
00:35:52,370 --> 00:35:55,710
Scott: Download a new copy of Notepad.exe and replace the old copy.

538
00:35:56,551 --> 00:36:06,030
Rym: So what I did is I used Notepad++, which I also had installed, just to write my little note to myself, and I decided to deal with it after the trip.

539
00:36:07,111 --> 00:36:17,670
Rym: So then I do the trip, and I'm in work, and I'm doing a presentation with the laptop, and I hit next slide at one point, and the slide just won't open.

540
00:36:17,810 --> 00:36:20,014
Rym: Like it just won't go to the next slide in a presentation.

541
00:36:23,119 --> 00:36:30,250
Rym: I restart PowerPoint, and I can go to any slide in the presentation, except that one, and it just won't load.

542
00:36:31,030 --> 00:36:32,115
Scott: Does it have Notepad in it?

543
00:36:34,304 --> 00:36:35,830
Scott: Is it a Notepad embed slide?

544
00:36:36,090 --> 00:36:42,142
Rym: So I do a Windows reinstall, like the refresh, like just redo all the Windows shit, and everything's fine again.

545
00:36:42,763 --> 00:36:46,350
Rym: Fast forward a couple weeks, and Notepad won't open again.

546
00:36:47,672 --> 00:36:50,430
Rym: So long story short, at this point- Isn't there anything in the Windows logs?

547
00:36:51,590 --> 00:37:01,570
Rym: Yes, so in the Windows logs, there are occasional I/O errors, but they're not correlated to anything, and the problem is, Windows logs have a lot of errors like that if you look at them, if you're not used to them.

548
00:37:02,071 --> 00:37:08,870
Rym: But I successfully, and to my credit, I'm very proud of this, I immediately diagnosed the problem at that moment.

549
00:37:09,470 --> 00:37:17,090
Rym: And what the problem ended up being, the SSD, which was a early generation solid state drive, had hit its end of life.

550
00:37:17,551 --> 00:37:23,461
Rym: And the way SSDs fail is they will have write failures over time.

551
00:37:23,501 --> 00:37:34,801
Rym: Like you will not be able to write to them anymore, or you will not be able to read from certain parts of them, because you can only write to segments of SSDs a limited number of times before they fail.

552
00:37:35,101 --> 00:37:40,270
Rym: And I had just pushed that SSD so long that it ran out of writes.

553
00:37:40,570 --> 00:37:45,600
Scott: Yeah, that's not something breaking- I mean it is breaking, but it's basically wearing out, right?

554
00:37:45,620 --> 00:37:50,730
Scott: That's like I wore out the tires on my car, it's like that's not a flat tire, that's not an exploded tire, that's not right.

555
00:37:51,511 --> 00:37:55,410
Scott: That is, the tires are only expected to last so long, and so they did.

556
00:37:56,051 --> 00:38:08,490
Rym: Yeah, but I guess I'd say the technology failure there was primarily that this was an era where SSDs on a consumer device would not tell you they had failed in any reasonable way.

557
00:38:09,570 --> 00:38:10,771
Rym: Not like modern ones do.

558
00:38:11,051 --> 00:38:16,376
Rym: Like now, I'll know if my SSD fails, but back then, nothing to do except replace the SSD.

559
00:38:19,198 --> 00:38:19,819
Rym: Should we alternate?

560
00:38:19,839 --> 00:38:20,719
Rym: You have more failures?

561
00:38:20,739 --> 00:38:22,781
Rym: Because I have a list of 10.

562
00:38:22,781 --> 00:38:23,542
Scott: Oh yeah, we got plenty.

563
00:38:24,463 --> 00:38:32,812
Scott: So, the very first time I experienced broken technology was our family had an Atari 2600.

564
00:38:32,812 --> 00:38:40,640
Scott: And I think that my parents and my aunts and uncles bought it together somehow, but it was in our house.

565
00:38:41,501 --> 00:38:48,151
Scott: And I used it, and then it just sort of didn't work, like you put games in and turned it on and they didn't play.

566
00:38:49,233 --> 00:38:53,740
Scott: And I was like four, so what the fuck did I know?

567
00:38:53,740 --> 00:38:59,240
Rym: 90% of the time that was always caused by the wall wart was busted.

568
00:38:59,901 --> 00:39:00,522
Scott: Who knows?

569
00:39:01,625 --> 00:39:07,520
Scott: But regardless, we just sort of stopped using it, and I don't know where it went, probably a garbage somewhere.

570
00:39:07,900 --> 00:39:08,217
Rym: I just gave up?

571
00:39:09,061 --> 00:39:16,398
Scott: I mean, I don't know, I was four, what the hell, but it was just the disappointment I had of going from having an Atari 2600 to...

572
00:39:17,260 --> 00:39:18,563
Rym: Oh my god, I can imagine that.

573
00:39:19,124 --> 00:39:25,840
Rym: Had that happened to my family, that would have been an all hands on deck, like Uncle Mike would have gotten the soldering iron out, like they would have fixed it no matter what.

574
00:39:26,261 --> 00:39:32,660
Scott: Right, it's like I didn't have another game console or any kind of device until like the NES several years later.

575
00:39:33,960 --> 00:39:37,050
Scott: And other people, I was later to the NES than other people, right?

576
00:39:37,130 --> 00:39:39,920
Scott: My light gun was a red light gun, other people had the gray one.

577
00:39:44,101 --> 00:39:49,680
Rym: The inscrutable failure that you don't even have the opportunity or the tools to investigate is sometimes the most frustrating.

578
00:39:50,201 --> 00:39:57,900
Scott: Yeah, I mean when I was a kid we had NES, you know, it would sometimes not work, we'd blow in the cartridge, we'd try to clean the cartridge, we'd try to clean the inside of the NES.

579
00:39:58,380 --> 00:40:03,880
Scott: But a lot, I think, it was probably like the pins not making good contact clean or not, they probably got loose.

580
00:40:05,381 --> 00:40:07,480
Scott: And it's like we didn't know how to fix that or try.

581
00:40:09,484 --> 00:40:11,060
Rym: We fixed a lot of stuff like that over the years.

582
00:40:11,100 --> 00:40:13,560
Scott: I have my NES in the closet, it's not very reliable.

583
00:40:14,680 --> 00:40:25,100
Rym: So, the year was, it was 2000, 2001, Scott and I, our freshmen living at RIT, we're not living together yet, this is right before we moved in together.

584
00:40:25,941 --> 00:40:27,499
Rym: And then that era lasted a long time.

585
00:40:28,000 --> 00:40:37,040
Rym: But, you know, I built a computer that I spent about $4000 on, I cashed in most of my bonds to buy this, to build this computer before I went to college.

586
00:40:37,200 --> 00:40:46,700
Rym: And I built an Athlon K7 800, 800 megahertz computer, brought it to college with an 8-bit motherboard.

587
00:40:48,200 --> 00:40:57,160
Rym: Just as the year is ending, and where everyone's going home, you left, everyone we knew left, I was like one of the last people from the original FRC hanging out on campus.

588
00:40:57,861 --> 00:41:01,840
Rym: And, like, the next day my parents are going to pick me up and drive me back home for the summer.

589
00:41:02,703 --> 00:41:04,780
Rym: And I'm playing Counter Strike and my computer crashed.

590
00:41:05,722 --> 00:41:08,500
Rym: And when I tried to reboot it, I couldn't get it to reboot.

591
00:41:09,280 --> 00:41:15,300
Rym: And I was worried, oh no, I've been overclocking, maybe finally fate has caught up to me.

592
00:41:15,700 --> 00:41:21,440
Rym: So I reboot in, like, safe mode, I disable all my, like, fucking overclock bullshit, and I try to boot the computer again.

593
00:41:21,961 --> 00:41:25,340
Rym: And it booted for a little while, but then it crashed again.

594
00:41:26,201 --> 00:41:35,740
Rym: And I was able to diagnose this one pretty quick too, because when a computer won't boot, and you can't even get into the BIOS, there's a limited number of things that are likely wrong.

595
00:41:35,780 --> 00:41:45,480
Rym: And I open the case, and I look at the motherboard, and you know how sometimes when a car is broken and you open the hood, and even if you don't know anything about cars, you're like, well that shouldn't look like that.

596
00:41:47,182 --> 00:41:52,260
Rym: I open the case, and I see black goop has come out of, like, ten capacitors on the motherboard.

597
00:41:53,780 --> 00:42:10,340
Rym: Because I had fallen victim to the problem that afflicted a lot of capacitors in that era where a specific Chinese company bootlegged a capacitor fluid, and that capacitor fluid was not correct, and any capacitor that used that fluid fucking blew up at some point.

598
00:42:12,162 --> 00:42:13,184
Rym: And there's no way to fix that.

599
00:42:13,204 --> 00:42:21,980
Rym: I was going to desolder, like, ten capacitors on an AMD motherboard in the year 2001.

600
