Scott: I don't want it because if I buy a 60 hertz monitor now and then I buy a 7080 in a few years now, I can't make most use of the 7080 without with the 60 hertz.

812
00:54:34,513 --> 00:54:35,774
Rym: And I got these Dell ultra sharp.

813
00:54:35,794 --> 00:54:38,456
Rym: So I'm going to try to hold out at least like five more years before I upgrade.

814
00:54:38,536 --> 00:54:41,398
Scott: But the one on the right, the more refurbished one that I got for free.

815
00:54:41,418 --> 00:54:44,420
Scott: This thing better last me at least until 2030.

816
00:54:44,420 --> 00:54:56,729
Rym: So speaking of money longer and it was a problem with these two Dell ultra sharps, actually, this was one of the most infuriating hardware problems I have ever had to troubleshoot that I was not being paid to troubleshoot by my job.

817
00:54:56,749 --> 00:54:58,410
Rym: I'm not going to talk about that one.

818
00:54:58,430 --> 00:54:59,090
Rym: That's a whole other.

819
00:54:59,310 --> 00:55:00,291
Rym: That's not a tech failure.

820
00:55:00,351 --> 00:55:05,574
Rym: That's the difference between 802.2 and ethernet.

821
00:55:05,614 --> 00:55:10,418
Rym: Anyway, so one day, you know, I'm using these two Dell ultra sharps.

822
00:55:10,458 --> 00:55:13,780
Rym: I just gotten them and one of them won't turn on like one will turn on.

823
00:55:13,800 --> 00:55:14,481
Rym: And one won't.

824
00:55:15,221 --> 00:55:16,743
Rym: So I'm like, ah, shit.

825
00:55:17,303 --> 00:55:20,787
Rym: So I swapped the cables and I do the usual troubleshooting.

826
00:55:21,087 --> 00:55:22,969
Rym: And one of the monitors just seems like it's fucked.

827
00:55:23,570 --> 00:55:25,431
Rym: So I'm extremely unhappy about this.

828
00:55:25,712 --> 00:55:28,194
Rym: So I set it aside and I decide to deal with it later.

829
00:55:28,815 --> 00:55:33,740
Rym: And like a few hours later, I go to like resume my troubleshooting and it works fine.

830
00:55:34,761 --> 00:55:35,601
Rym: So I don't think much about it.

831
00:55:36,482 --> 00:55:39,164
Rym: The next day, the same one stops working again.

832
00:55:39,945 --> 00:55:41,607
Rym: So fuck, I start troubleshooting.

833
00:55:41,667 --> 00:55:47,211
Rym: I have plugged in, unplugged, swapping cables, swapping every possible thing, putting them through adapters.

834
00:55:47,632 --> 00:55:50,454
Rym: And then a few hours later, starts working again.

835
00:55:51,195 --> 00:55:51,455
Scott: Hmm.

836
00:55:52,216 --> 00:55:57,160
Rym: So I swap which cable is pointing at which one and nothing changes.

837
00:55:58,400 --> 00:56:03,424
Rym: This happens a few times, but then the left monitor fails and the right one's fine.

838
00:56:04,185 --> 00:56:04,385
Scott: Hmm.

839
00:56:04,785 --> 00:56:07,627
Rym: So but I haven't swapped anything during that iteration.

840
00:56:08,268 --> 00:56:09,669
Rym: And the long story.

841
00:56:09,849 --> 00:56:23,380
Rym: to make a long story short, I was only able to troubleshoot this because when I was describing the insanity of this problem, a few of our friends and you might have been one of the people who mentioned it said, oh, that sounds like the DisplayPort pin 20 problem.

842
00:56:25,161 --> 00:56:26,743
Scott: I don't think I was the one who knew that.

843
00:56:26,923 --> 00:56:31,590
Scott: Yeah, maybe we both learned about it, but basically, I think I learned about it when you hit.

844
00:56:31,630 --> 00:56:38,380
Scott: it might have been you might have found the answer while I was describing or if I or if I did know it, I forgot that I knew it.

845
00:56:39,181 --> 00:56:48,129
Rym: Basically, the problem was a lot of DisplayPort cables were bootleg and wired up wrong and they wire this one pin all the way through.

846
00:56:48,529 --> 00:56:49,610
Rym: But that pin is for power.

847
00:56:50,230 --> 00:57:01,120
Rym: So if you have a DisplayPort cable like that, then the monitor is sending 500 milliamps at three point three volts over that cable at your GPU.

848
00:57:01,901 --> 00:57:07,408
Rym: But your GPU is sending 500 milliamps or three point three volts at the monitor.

849
00:57:07,889 --> 00:57:16,720
Rym: And what are the ramifications of this kind of random, depending on a lot of factors like there's no way to troubleshoot that shit.

850
00:57:17,100 --> 00:57:18,082
Rym: That's just insane.

851
00:57:18,142 --> 00:57:19,104
Rym: There's no way to fix that.

852
00:57:19,425 --> 00:57:20,707
Rym: It can cause permanent damage.

853
00:57:21,048 --> 00:57:23,733
Rym: It was basically it was causing the monitor to get into a bad state.

854
00:57:24,074 --> 00:57:27,320
Rym: But then eventually, I guess some capacitor were drained and that would be fine again.

855
00:57:28,100 --> 00:57:35,420
Scott: So the way I know that that is not the problem I'm experiencing, because this is like the top quality DisplayPort cable that came with.

856
00:57:35,921 --> 00:57:42,996
Rym: I mean, the way to avoid that problem, don't buy DisplayPort cables off Amazon or Wish or whatever, Temu or make sure your monitors will come with them.

857
00:57:43,296 --> 00:57:43,917
Scott: Use that one.

858
00:57:43,938 --> 00:57:44,439
Scott: Right.

859
00:57:44,519 --> 00:57:45,080
Scott: And you'll be good.

860
00:57:45,240 --> 00:57:53,660
Rym: So I went through every DSP cable I owned, determined which ones had the 20, the pin 20 problem, cut them in two and threw them away one by one.

861
00:57:54,221 --> 00:57:54,621
Scott: That's right.

862
00:57:55,542 --> 00:57:56,944
Scott: I had a similar problem, actually.

863
00:57:57,004 --> 00:58:01,551
Scott: The TV that I had before I moved to this apartment, I gave it away for free when I moved.

864
00:58:01,952 --> 00:58:07,880
Scott: It had a problem where I'm pretty sure it was capacitor related, where you turn the TV off and it worked.

865
00:58:08,360 --> 00:58:09,041
Scott: It turns off.

866
00:58:09,382 --> 00:58:09,602
Scott: Right.

867
00:58:10,183 --> 00:58:15,871
Scott: But when you turned it off, you had to wait some unknown amount of time before attempting to turn it on again.

868
00:58:15,911 --> 00:58:21,520
Scott: If you turned it off and then turned it on again too quickly, the red light would blink as if it was attempting to turn on.

869
00:58:21,981 --> 00:58:23,803
Scott: And then it would hold steady as if it was on.

870
00:58:23,823 --> 00:58:25,265
Rym: All right.

871
00:58:25,305 --> 00:58:26,187
Rym: I remember this.

872
00:58:26,727 --> 00:58:28,430
Scott: But then it wouldn't be on.

873
00:58:28,811 --> 00:58:31,895
Scott: It would just be a black screen that the display wouldn't actually turn.

874
00:58:31,915 --> 00:58:35,260
Scott: It would still be off as far as you could tell, even though it thought it was on.

875
00:58:35,380 --> 00:58:41,168
Scott: So I think the board probably thought it was on, but the display was not on and the backlight wasn't on either.

876
00:58:41,588 --> 00:58:50,100
Scott: And so if this happened, the only thing you could do was unplug the television and wait an indeterminate amount of time.

877
00:58:50,280 --> 00:58:50,480
Scott: Yeah.

878
00:58:51,201 --> 00:58:54,406
Scott: For I presume capacitors to drain or the whatever.

879
00:58:54,506 --> 00:58:54,947
Scott: Who knows?

880
00:58:55,487 --> 00:58:58,211
Scott: And then you could plug the TV back in and turn it on.

881
00:58:58,872 --> 00:59:01,896
Scott: If you tried to do it quickly, like unplug, replug, that wasn't going to work.

882
00:59:01,916 --> 00:59:04,540
Scott: You had to wait some amount of unknown amount of time.

883
00:59:05,440 --> 00:59:08,005
Scott: And then you turn the TV on again and then you'd be good to go.

884
00:59:08,165 --> 00:59:09,026
Scott: And just you just couldn't.

885
00:59:09,066 --> 00:59:12,412
Scott: The only thing is you couldn't turn it on quickly after turning it off.

886
00:59:12,432 --> 00:59:13,093
Scott: You turn it off.

887
00:59:13,374 --> 00:59:17,280
Scott: You had to wait before turning it on and you would never experience the problem.

888
00:59:17,380 --> 00:59:23,233
Scott: So when I gave it away for free, I let the people know that I never tried to repair it or diagnose it.

889
00:59:23,354 --> 00:59:25,980
Scott: I just didn't turn it on quickly after turning it off.

890
00:59:27,041 --> 00:59:31,486
Rym: The most infuriating thing I have ever troubleshot was my own fault.

891
00:59:31,987 --> 00:59:43,300
Rym: And I'm not going to describe how I troubleshot it because it was very hard to troubleshoot and involves a lot of talk of DHCP and low level networking and doing taps.

892
00:59:43,660 --> 00:59:44,802
Rym: But I can.

893
00:59:44,942 --> 00:59:47,346
Rym: I can explain the manifestation.

894
00:59:47,386 --> 00:59:57,420
Rym: The manifestation was that you have a computer and you plug it into a switch with an ethernet cable and you get a link light, but you cannot establish a connection over that cable.

895
00:59:57,440 --> 00:59:58,421
Rym: Like just nothing works.

896
00:59:59,222 --> 01:00:00,264
Rym: But there is a link light.

897
01:00:00,704 --> 01:00:01,565
Rym: The switch lights up.

898
01:00:02,126 --> 01:00:03,948
Rym: The mother of network card lights up.

899
01:00:04,429 --> 01:00:05,390
Rym: There is a connection.

900
01:00:05,570 --> 01:00:07,853
Rym: The computer detects ethernet cable is there.

901
01:00:07,873 --> 01:00:11,157
Rym: And if you try to use it, it just kind of doesn't work.

902
01:00:11,217 --> 01:00:13,460
Rym: And like the packets don't all get there.

903
01:00:15,621 --> 01:00:27,400
Rym: I caused this because I made ethernet cables for my brother and all the people in his dorm when he moved into college with that same crimper that I bought to make all our cables when we were at RIT.

904
01:00:28,461 --> 01:00:43,297
Rym: And one of the cables that I made, I made I must have just been moving too fast and I shorted two pins on that cable inside the head, like inside the connector in the exact way.

905
01:00:43,317 --> 01:00:46,620
Rym: That is how a link light is detected.

906
01:00:47,301 --> 01:00:49,066
Rym: So I made a trap.

907
01:00:49,488 --> 01:00:53,800
Rym: I made a cable that will always show a link light, but could never be used to send data.

908
01:00:57,382 --> 01:00:57,422
Scott: OK.

909
01:00:57,442 --> 01:00:58,204
Scott: That's not a fail.

910
01:00:58,264 --> 01:00:59,447
Scott: That's just you fucking up.

911
01:00:59,548 --> 01:00:59,768
Rym: Yeah.

912
01:00:59,929 --> 01:01:01,293
Rym: Well, the hardware was.

913
01:01:01,313 --> 01:01:03,920
Rym: I guess the hardware was failed from day one.

914
01:01:06,261 --> 01:01:07,883
Rym: I feel like your monitor problems are.

915
01:01:07,963 --> 01:01:10,567
Rym: I bet that's just a problem with the hardware in that monitor.

916
01:01:11,408 --> 01:01:13,091
Rym: Maybe the firmware, something like that.

917
01:01:15,654 --> 01:01:16,916
Rym: Anyway, we've gone on for over an hour.

918
01:01:16,936 --> 01:01:19,460
Rym: I feel like that's enough failures to talk about.

919
01:01:19,941 --> 01:01:20,383
Scott: That's enough.

920
01:01:20,724 --> 01:01:21,729
Rym: Yeah.

921
01:01:21,769 --> 01:01:22,713
Rym: Is there a moral to this?

922
01:01:22,774 --> 01:01:24,079
Rym: I feel like the moral is.

923
01:01:24,400 --> 01:01:27,147
Scott: The moral is you need to pick books for the book club.

924
01:01:27,228 --> 01:01:27,488
Scott: Yes.

925
01:01:27,949 --> 01:01:30,736
Rym: Also, the moral is basic.

926
01:01:30,796 --> 01:01:32,240
Rym: troubleshooting skills are universal.

927
01:01:33,840 --> 01:01:35,886
Scott: And sometimes shit really does just break in.

928
01:01:35,926 --> 01:01:38,674
Scott: It's not Rym breaking it on purpose to make an excuse to buy something new.

929
01:01:38,794 --> 01:01:40,680
Rym: Just like sometimes your thing crash.

930
01:01:40,940 --> 01:01:45,171
Scott: Although sometimes it is Rym breaking something on purpose so he doesn't feel bad about buying something new like you.

931
01:01:45,212 --> 01:01:46,716
Rym: You definitely want a new monitor here.

932
01:01:46,796 --> 01:01:48,360
Rym: I feel like I do not have a monitor.

933
01:01:48,460 --> 01:01:50,710
Scott: This monitor is so great if it only works.

934
01:01:51,453 --> 01:01:53,040
Scott: It was like the best monitor when it was working.

935
01:01:58,540 --> 01:02:00,889
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

936
01:02:00,950 --> 01:02:03,460
Rym: Special thanks to DJ Pretzel for the opening theme.

937
01:02:03,660 --> 01:02:09,680
Scott: Be sure to visit our website at frontrowclue.com for show notes, discussion, links and more.

938
